{"learned": "We have two theories about why consumers might adopt lyocell/modal: T1 (premium sustainable differentiation) and T2 (status/ethical signal). Evidence shows consumer demand for sustainability information (PEFC survey) and that Lenzing already launched Tencel as a B2B2C brand, but no direct proof of willingness to pay a premium. No searches remain, so we cannot find new data. Adding a third theory would introduce an alternative mechanism (functional performance) that could be tested against the existing ones. A test could then be designed where T1 and T2 predict higher purchase intent at premium prices, while T3 predicts purchase intent driven by functional attributes at a smaller premium or a",
"uncertain": "Whether consumers are willing to pay a premium for lyocell/modal based on sustainability or status, and whether a functional performance proposition could also drive demand. The existing evidence does not directly quantify willingness to pay or isolate the drivers.",
"why_next": "Adding a third theory (T3) about functional benefits would create a clearer test where the three theories predict different purchase intent levels under distinct price points. This allows us to propose an affordable experiment (within \u20ac20k, 12 weeks) that can discriminate between the mechanisms. We need to add T3 first, then assess existing evidence against it, and finally propose a test.","call": {"name": "add_theory","arguments": {"claim":"Consumers will adopt lyocell/modal products if they perceive superior tactile and performance attributes (e.g., softness, breathability, durability) and are willing to pay a modest premium for these functional benefits, independent of sustainability signaling.","who_benefits":"End\u2011users seeking comfort and performance; Lenzing benefits by commanding a premium based on product performance claims and retaining higher margins through direct\u2011to\u2011consumer or branded sales.","why_behaviour_changes":"Market research on textile attributes shows consumers prioritize feel and durability; lyocell and modal are known for these qualities, creating a functional differentiation from conventional viscose.","how_value_is_captured":"Lenzing can price lyocell/modal based on performance benefits, sell through retail channels emphasizing tactile qualities, and capture margin by positioning as a premium functional fiber rather than just a sustainable one.","assumptions":["Consumers associate lyocell/modal with superior softness and durability.","Lenzing can communicate functional benefits clearly to consumers.","Retail partners will stock products marketed on performance attributes."],"predictions":["In a test offering a lyocell/modal product at a price 10% above conventional viscose, purchase intent will be driven primarily by stated functional benefits and will be at least as high as for a comparable viscose product, but lower than a ","premium sustainability positioning at 20% above viscose."]}} }
